7.10.m:
The aśvattha is the seat of the gods
in the third heaven from here;
there is the sight of immortality;
from there kuṣṭha was born;
he […]

7.14.m:
Agni has full life;
he has full life through the trees of the forest.
May he, having full life, make me have full life!

The ocean has full life;
it has full life through the rivers.
May it, having full life, make me have full life!

The sacred formulation has full life;
it has full life through the Vedic students.
May it, having full life, make me have full life!

Indra has full life;
he has full life through heroic power.
May he, having full life, make me have full life!

The gods have full life;
they have full life through immortality.
May they, having full life, make me have full life!

Prajāpati has full life;
he has full life through his creatures.
May he, having full life, make me have full life!

7.15.m:
May the sacrificial fee protect me from the right;
may the sacrificial fee protect [me] from the left;
may it protect [me] from piercing from behind,
from the weapon of every Bhava!

7.16.m:
May Agni with the Vasus protect me from the east.
In him I step, in him I take refuge;
into that stronghold I go forward.
May he protect me, may he guard me;
to him I surrender myself. Svāhā!

May Sūrya with Heaven and Earth protect me from the western direction.
In him I step, in him I take refuge;
into that stronghold I go forward.
May he protect me, may he guard me;
to him I surrender myself. Svāhā!

May the waters possessed of plants protect me from this direction.
In them I step, in them I take refuge;
into that stronghold I go forward.
May they protect me, may they guard me;
to them I surrender myself. Svāhā!

May Bṛhaspati with the All-gods protect me from the upward direction.
In him I step, in him I take refuge;
into that stronghold I go forward.
May he protect me, may he guard me;
to him I surrender myself. Svāhā!

7.18.m:
Having run beyond, O outrunning charms,
strike down with Indra's strength!
Crush [him] as a wolf crushes a sheep;
let him not escape alive from you;
tie up his breath!

O Indra, slayer of Vṛtra,
fierce one, pierce his vital spots!
Trample him right here;
O mighty one, I am your ally!
We hold on behind you, O Indra;
may we be in your favor!

8.1.m:
One is beyond this expanse of space;
beyond the one is another, hard to attain.
That I, knowing, proclaim to you, O Varuṇa:
let the Paṇis have their words cast down,
let the defiled Dāsas slink low!

I approach your praise-songs that bring strengthening;
give me that which is ungiven!
You are our allied friend of seven paces;
equal is our kinship, O Varuṇa, equal our origin.

8.2.m:
Āliki and Vili are the father
and who is your mother;
we know your kinship on all sides;
O sapless one, what will you do?

8.3.m:
You are the protection of men,
the protection of cows;
for the protection of horses, of coursers,
you have taken your stand.

8.8.m:
Heart with heart,
tuft of hair together with tuft of hair;
release the wry smile with waters;
may the shine of my heel come together.

8.9.m:
With the sacred word as foremost, heroic powers were assembled;
the sacred word as the eldest extended the sky in the beginning;
the sacred word was born first among beings:
who is able to vie with that sacred word?

The sacred word is these two, heaven and earth;
the sacred word is these seven rivers;
the sacred word is all these Ādityas;
upon the sacred word the gods attend.

The sacred word the Brahmin speaks;
with the sacred word the night settles down;
what belongs to Savitṛ was born from the sacred word;
through the sacred word fire shines out.

By the sacred word the herbs abide;
by the sacred word the rains pour down;
the sacred word is this whole living world,
as far as the sun looks out.

The sacred word is the Hotṛ, the sacred word the sacrifice;
by the sacred word the sacrificial posts are measured;
the Adhvaryu was born from the sacred word;
through the sacred word the oblation rises up.

The sacred word is the [ladles] filled with ghee;
by the sacred word the altar is raised;
the sacred word is the threads of the sacrifice,
the priests who prepare the oblation.

The sacred word is the bull of auspicious seed;
the sacred word is the cows that prepare the oblation;
the sacred word is of the divine chariot,
which, well-adorned, wins.

By the sacred word the plow moves forward;
by the sacred word the chariot is yoked;
by the sacred word the two-footed man,
walking, does not falter.

Born from the sacred word are the ṛṣis,
born from the sacred word are also the nobles;
this sacred word was born from the sacred word;
from the sacred word [comes] the food of the desirous.

The sacred word is the Śūdras of the nobles,
and the sacred word is their settlement-dweller;
the sacred word is their auspicious seat;
by the sacred word are their assembly-sitters.

The sacred word is the boatmen, the sacred word is the Dāsas;
the sacred word are also these gamblers;
woman and man were born from the sacred word;
the women have also loved the sacred word.

The sacred word is the valleys, the slopes;
the trees, the mountains, the plants are the sacred word;
the sacred word has extended over all this,
between both heaven and earth.

The sacred word has become supreme, O Jātavedas;
let the thunderbolt crush the mighty sorcerer;
let Bhava and Śarva, impelled by me, unleash their burning missile
upon him for his slaughter.

8.10.m:
For I have brought water for you,
waters that sprinkle fortune;
whatever splendor of yours has departed
from the seeing of the face,
may the Aśvins, lotus-wreathed,
place that back in you.

8.11.m:
Take hold of the vigorous one;
winnow the one possessed of increase;
Indra brings the seed;
let Bhaga go forth as leader.

The ladles were winnowing-fans,
the sickles were the enclosing-sticks;
the plowmen were the Hotṛs;
the giver of seed was the lord of the oblation.

8.12.m:
Your name by birth is Reed;
become the vessel of surā;
for an auspicious friend to you was
the sweet vikankata-tree.

8.13.m:
From the sea the wave rich in honey has arisen;
together with the silent prayer it attained immortality;
which is the secret name of ghee:
the tongue of the gods, the navel of immortality.

Let us proclaim the name of ghee;
let us uphold it with reverences at this sacrifice;
the four-horned buffalo bellowed this
to the sacred word being praised.

Four horns, three feet are his;
two heads, seven hands are his.
Bound in three ways, the bull bellows aloud;
the great god has entered mortals.

These flow from the ocean of the heart;
with a hundred folds, not to be seen by the deceiver,
I look upon the streams of ghee;
in their midst is the golden reed.

Like fair women smiling as they go to an assembly,
they flow toward Agni;
the streams of ghee reach the kindling sticks;
enjoying them, Jātavedas longs for them.

Flow toward the fair praise, the race for cattle;
bestow upon us auspicious treasures.
Lead this sacrifice of ours to the gods;
the streams of ghee clarify themselves, rich in honey.

8.14.m:
Two of different form move for their own purpose;
the one and the other suckle the calf.
The golden one is with the one, possessing ancestral offering;
the bright, splendid one is seen with the other.

He stretches upward his arms like Savitṛ;
striding, terrible, he reaches both borders.
He drives off his bright garment from all sides;
he abandons his old clothes for new ones from the mothers.

He makes a desert into a stream, a path for the wave;
with bright waves he reaches the earth.
He holds all ancient things in his bellies;
he moves within the new mothers.

8.17.m:
Mitra stepped up with the earth;
to that fortress I lead you.
Enter it, penetrate into it;
may it grant you shelter and armor!

Prajāpati stepped up with [his] creatures;
to that fortress I lead you.
Enter it, penetrate into it;
may it grant you shelter and armor!

8.20.m:
With a golden wheel
the house of good fortune is covered;
I uncover it by the sacred word.
Grant it to me, O Aśvins;
grant it to me, O lotus-wreathed pair!

9.1.m:
May he sacrifice to the greatness of this Agni;
he indeed, amid delightful offerings,
is the most attentive to the good, the greatest bestower of wealth.

O divine Hotṛs, praise this sacrificial rite of ours
upon Agni's tongue;
make our sacrifice well-offered!

That wonderful, abundant wealth [grant] to us;
O Tvaṣṭṛ, untie for us good hero-strength,
the increase of wealth, the lineage.

O Lord of the Forest, release [it],
rejoicing in yourself, for the gods;
may Agni as slaughterer prepare the oblation.

O Agni Jātavedas, pronounce svāhā!
[Make] the portion for Indra;
may all the gods enjoy this oblation!

9.3.m:
On the tenth night I know the remedy
for white leprosy by means of the plant;
from here I have dug it up with the shovel;
I make your white leprosy disappear.

It becomes reddish-yellow in appearance,
and mottled to look upon;
O white leprosy, perish far from here;
I will burn you away with the plant!

From your head, from the shoulders,
from the forehead, away from the ears,
with the plant quickened by rain
I destroy your white leprosy;
your own color returns to you;
non-hostile is the plant together [with you].

From your neck-tendons, from the cervical vertebrae,
from the breast-bones, from the spine,
[…]

From your shoulder blades, from your two arms,
from the forearms, away from the two hands,
[…]

From your ribs, from the two flanks,
from the hips, away from the buttocks,
[…]

From your knee-caps,
from the heels, from the forefeet,
with the plant quickened by rain
I destroy your white leprosy;
your own color returns to you;
non-hostile is the plant together [with you].

9.4.m:
United be your heart,
and united your mind;
united is Agni, your god;
together attend upon the realm.

Agree together, having one heart;
be all of one mind!
Destroyed is your wrath, worn away your jealousy;
live together with good fortune!

Together be your grains,
and united your cattle;
together on earth be the plants;
together let there be herbs for you.

Together be consecration, together sacrifice;
let your marriage be together;
let wanton girls dance together;
let your women sit together.

Together be your heroic powers;
together subjugate others;
together launch the feathered arrow,
the missile, against another!

I bring together completely
[your minds], like herds of cattle together;
united be your mind;
follow the foremost understanding!

This which is their heart,
let that be in their heart;
moreover, what is their heart,
let that rest in their heart;
united be your mind;
follow the best understanding!

9.5.m:
With three feet he ascended to heaven;
a foot of him was here again;
thence he strode out in all directions,
over that which eats and that which does not eat.

Such are his greatnesses,
and greater still is the Puruṣa;
a quarter of him is all beings;
three-quarters of him are the immortal in heaven.

The brāhmaṇa was his mouth;
the rājanya became his two arms;
his middle part was what the vaiśya is;
from his two feet the śūdra was born.

From him horses were born,
and whatever [beasts] have two rows of teeth;
cattle indeed were born from him;
from him were born goats and sheep.

From that sacrifice in which everything was offered
the verses and the chants were born;
the meters indeed were born from him;
the sacrificial formula was born from him.

The moon was born from his mind;
from his eye the sun was born;
from his ear wind and breath [were born];
from his mouth fire was born.

[From his navel] came the intermediate space;
from his head heaven arose;
from his two feet the earth; the directions from his ear;
thus they fashioned the worlds.

9.6.m:
I have slain the blood-eaters;
they have grasped the belly-eaters;
I have smashed the eggs of all
the unseen ones who dwell in the earth.

9.7.m:
Let cold waters, let cold winds blow hither!
By snow is Agni covered;
by snow is Agni encompassed.

When the gods checked you,
you ran to the sea;
snow struck the cattle-driver;
snow [struck] the axle, snow the cart-cover.

By the sacred word I seize your name,
abode by abode, joint by joint;
Śītikā is your mother by name;
Jalāṣa is your father by name.

Raw is your name, O plant;
by that name I have grasped you;
O sons of Agastya,
do not burn my men!

The stars have fallen from heaven;
the waters of the sea from the torrent;
the golden vessel, yellowish-brown,
encompassed with avakā-plants—
with that we extinguish Agni.

I extinguish the flame of Agni;
propitious let him burn, let him not consume!
Seized are heaven and earth;
seized is the earthly realm.

9.8.m:
The gods plowed this barley mixed with honey
upon the Sarasvatī, in a boat;
Indra of a hundred crafts was the master of the plow;
the Maruts of good gifts were the plowmen.

Lean, you two won this
honey of the bee, O Aśvins;
from that the barley sprouted up;
it became the spoiler of poison.

The steed tore away
and brought honey from the bee;
[…]

Kairaṇḍā by name is the pond
upon the vomit of the wolf;
from that the barley sprouted up;
it became the spoiler of poison.

9.10.m:
Poison is placed in the sacred fig tree,
poison is placed in the skull;
from the stone was born Taimāta,
the foremost poison-spoiler.

9.11.m:
Bhava mounted onto you,
Rudra brought your stalk;
Night watched over you;
as such, you were born into being.

Heat was your rain,
Agni became shade and heat;
the cloud was your embryonic cover;
as such, you were born into being.

The ageless gods deposited
nectar among mortals;
I took the tip of it;
that is your poison-spoiler.

9.12.m:
The god has indeed flown down to the hospitable reception, to Agni,
agreeing with both kinds of fathers;
the great mortal has now come to the feed;
having gathered the Ādityas, the fire-carrier settled down.

They enter into the lying Puruṣa;
the breaths, having entered, do not praise him;
they, well-disposed to us by night,
guard us by day, unangered.

O Lord of Cattle, be gracious to our cattle!
O Sarva, tear away those approaching;
do not injure our vital breaths!

The gods were arrayed at Prajāpati's side,
and the Ādityas held themselves ready;
Pūṣan stretched out in the rays;
the Āditya is Viṣṇu in striding;
he ascends the ascent in heaven.

O Maruts of true ordinances,
come to us with rain for our aid;
quicken [us] in the afternoons!

Indra, the king, the lord of heaven,
stands measuring out the day;
may he arrange these directions for us!

9.13.m:
From near to you, from far;
from far to you, from near—
be here! Do not go now;
do not follow those who have gone before!
I bind your breath of life firmly.

9.14.m:
With breath, O Agni, with sight unite this man;
spur him on with a body, with strength!
You know of immortality; do not die;
let him not become a dweller in the earth!

9.15.m:
King Soma, not bearing a grudge,
gave back first the wife of the brāhmaṇa;
Varuṇa and Mitra were followers;
Agni the Hotṛ led her, taking her by the hand.

9.17.m:
Agni indeed is our leader;
Soma is called the heir;
Indra is the conqueror of curses:
that is the truth established with the gods.

9.18.m:
Like a poisoned arrow, O lord of men,
like a viper, O lord of cattle;
she is the poisoned arrow of the brāhmaṇa;
with her he pierces the scorner.

The brāhmaṇas have sharp arrows, they possess missiles;
the arrow-target which they shoot at is not in vain;
pursuing with ascetic heat and with wrath,
they split [him] down from afar with it.

9.19.m:
He assails a brāhmaṇa with his voice;
he harms a relative through thoughtlessness;
he plays false with a friend in truth—
the man whom the gods slay.

9.21.m:
Whoever should distribute a single-pot portion would distribute it after the Single Ṛṣi.
This indeed is the Single Ṛṣi, namely Agni.
He secures both the Single Ṛṣi and the world.
He burns like the Single Ṛṣi, he shines like the Single Ṛṣi, he becomes an eater of food like the Single Ṛṣi, who knows thus.
He who, knowing thus, should eat it, should mentally meditate on this very deity:
"With the eye of the Single Ṛṣi I look upon you;
with the hands of the Single Ṛṣi I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the Single Ṛṣi I eat you;
in the belly of the Single Ṛṣi I seat you."
As he would eat what is offered and sacrificed, even so does he eat it.

Whoever should distribute a two-pot portion would distribute it after in-breath and out-breath.
These indeed are in-breath and out-breath, namely Mātariśvan and Agni.
He secures both in-breath and out-breath, and the world.
He lives long, attains full life, does not die before old age, who […].
He who, knowing thus, should eat it, should mentally meditate on these two deities:
"With the eye of in-breath and out-breath I look upon you;
with the hands of in-breath and out-breath I take hold of you;
with the mouth of in-breath and out-breath I eat you;
in the belly of in-breath and out-breath […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a three-pot portion would distribute it after the three Trikadrukas.
These indeed are the three Trikadrukas, namely ṛks, sāmans, yajuses, [and] the brāhmaṇa.
He secures both the sacred word and the world.
He becomes possessed of brāhmaṇa luster, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of the sacred word I look upon you;
with the hands of the sacred word I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the sacred word I eat you;
with […] of the sacred word […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a four-pot portion would distribute it after the four wide spaces.
These indeed are the four wide spaces, namely the directions.
He secures both the directions and the world.
The directions arrange themselves for him, he becomes dear to the directions, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of the directions I look upon you;
with the hands of the directions I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the directions I eat you;
with […] of the directions […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a five-pot portion would distribute it after Vaiśvānara of five heads.
This indeed is Vaiśvānara of five heads, namely heaven and earth and Mātariśvan and Agni […].
He secures both Vaiśvānara and the world.
He burns like Vaiśvānara, he shines like Vaiśvānara, he becomes an eater of food like Vaiśvānara, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of Vaiśvānara I look upon you;
with the hands of Vaiśvānara I take hold of you;
with the mouth of Vaiśvānara I eat you;
with […] of Vaiśvānara […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a six-pot portion would distribute it after the six-paced gods.
These indeed are the six-paced gods, namely the seasons.
He secures both the seasons and the world.
The seasons arrange themselves for him; he is not cut off in the seasons; he becomes dear to the seasons, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on these deities:
"With the eye of the seasons I look upon you;
with the hands of the seasons I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the seasons I eat you;
with […] of the seasons […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a seven-pot portion would distribute it after the seven Ṛṣis.
These indeed are the seven Ṛṣis, namely prāṇa, apāna, and vyāna.
He secures both the Seven Ṛṣis and the world.
He lives long, he attains a full life, he does not die before old age, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on these deities:
"With the eye of the Seven Ṛṣis I look upon you;
with the hands of the Seven Ṛṣis I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the Seven Ṛṣis I eat you;
with […] of the Seven Ṛṣis […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute an eight-pot portion would distribute it after the eight-footed Virāj.
This indeed is the eight-footed Virāj, namely heaven and earth, waters and plants, wind and atmosphere, sun and moon.
He secures both the Virāj and the world.
He shines forth both in this world and in yonder world; they call him "bull of the Virāj", who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of Virāj I look upon you;
with the hands of Virāj I take hold of you;
with the mouth of Virāj I eat you;
with […] of Virāj […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a nine-pot portion would distribute it after the nine-paced gods.
These indeed are the nine-paced gods, namely the months.
He secures both the months and the world.
The months arrange themselves for him, he becomes dear to the months, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on these deities:
"With the eye of the months I look upon you;
with the hands of the months I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the months I eat you;
with […] of the months […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a ten-pot portion would distribute it after Iḍā the milch cow.
This indeed is Iḍā the milch cow, namely the sacrifice [and] the cattle.
He secures Iḍā the milch cow, the sacrifice, the world, and cattle.
[They] arrange themselves [for him], he becomes dear to Iḍā, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of Iḍā I look upon you;
with the hands of Iḍā I take hold of you;
with the mouth of Iḍā I eat you;
with […] of Iḍā […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute an eleven-pot portion would distribute it after Rohita.
This indeed is Rohita, namely Indra.
He secures both Indra and the world.
Possessed of virile power, he becomes dear to Indra, who […]
[…] should eat it, should mentally meditate on this deity:
"With the eye of Indra I look upon you;
with the hands of Indra I take hold of you;
with the mouth of Indra I eat you;
with […] of Indra […]"
[…]

Whoever should distribute a twelve-pot portion would distribute it after all the gods.
These indeed are the All-Gods, namely all this.
He secures both the All-Gods and the world.
The All-Gods arrange themselves for him; he becomes dear to the All-Gods, who knows thus.
He, knowing thus, who should eat it, should mentally meditate on these deities:
"With the eye of the All-Gods I look upon you;
with the hands of the All-Gods I take hold of you;
with the mouth of the All-Gods I eat you;
into the belly of the All-Gods I set you down."
As one would eat what is offered and sacrificed, even so does he eat it.

9.24.m:
Do not leave yakṣa-disease here;
do not scatter, [you who are] babbling.
Let merit be ours alone;
offense follows [you].

9.27.m:
Be first, O drum, to overcome the enemies;
speak on the back of the earth, shining much!
Allied with Indra, summon the warriors;
with friends, strike down the enemies!

9.28.m:
Let your lying down be full of sorrow;
let your sitting be full of sorrow;
let your mind be full of sorrow,
so that you will not take pleasure there,
nor turn your mind here—
fly forth […]!

You shall not take pleasure in assemblies;
let it burn you as you lie down;
let the drop prick you with a goad—
fly forth […]!

10.1.m:
Around have these led the cow,
around have they brought the fire;
they have made renown among the gods;
who will dare assail them?
